1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,360
August eighth, 2023.

2
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:08,320
Lahaina, Maui. At 6:37 in the morning, a
downed power line sparks a brush fire on

3
00:00:08,360 --> 00:00:12,760
the edge of town. By nine, firefighters
declare it contained.

4
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:15,530
By 3:30 in the afternoon, it is back.

5
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:19,560
That day, the wind is gusting at more than
eighty miles an hour.

6
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:23,770
At 4:46, the fire crosses the highway, and
enters Lahaina.

7
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:26,840
Maui has eighty outdoor warning sirens.

8
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:29,530
They exist for moments exactly like this.

9
00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:32,770
That afternoon, not one of them sounded.

10
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:37,370
By 5:45, the fire has reached the ocean.

11
00:00:37,520 --> 00:00:40,200
People are jumping into the water to
escape it.

12
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:43,400
The Coast Guard will pull seventeen of
them out of the sea.

13
00:00:43,440 --> 00:00:47,160
When it is over, more than two thousand
buildings are gone.

14
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:49,290
One hundred and two people are dead.

15
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:54,120
It is the deadliest wildfire in the United
States in more than a hundred years.

16
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:56,730
And the questions start almost
immediately.

17
00:00:57,040 --> 00:00:59,080
Why did the sirens stay silent?

18
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:00,800
Why did the hydrants fail?

19
00:01:00,840 --> 00:01:04,640
And how did a fire that was declared
contained at nine in the morning come

20
00:01:04,680 --> 00:01:07,400
back, and destroy an entire town?

21
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:10,650
This is the story of the day Lahaina
burned.

22
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:17,800
To understand how this happened, you have
to understand Lahaina.

23
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:22,360
For a time in the 1800s, this small town
on the west coast of Maui was the royal

24
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:24,600
capital of the Kingdom of Hawaiʻi.

25
00:01:24,640 --> 00:01:29,240
King Kamehameha the Third lived here, on a
tiny island in the middle of a spring-fed

26
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:31,160
pond called Mokuhinia.

27
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:33,680
Lahaina was a place of water.

28
00:01:33,720 --> 00:01:37,720
Streams ran down from the mountains,
through taro fields and fishponds, to the

29
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:40,480
sea. Then came the whaling ships.

30
00:01:40,520 --> 00:01:42,440
And after them, sugar.

31
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:45,840
Plantations diverted the mountain streams
to water their cane.

32
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:47,800
The royal pond dried up.

33
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:51,840
In 1919, it was filled in and turned into
a baseball field.

34
00:01:51,880 --> 00:01:56,200
By the twenty-first century, Lahaina was
one of the driest places in Hawaiʻi, with

35
00:01:56,240 --> 00:01:58,600
about fifteen inches of rain a year.

36
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:02,280
Front Street ran along the ocean, lined
with wooden buildings from the whaling

37
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:07,880
days. In the town square stood a banyan
tree, planted in 1873, that had grown to

38
00:02:07,920 --> 00:02:09,640
cover almost an acre.

39
00:02:09,680 --> 00:02:12,960
And on the hills above the town, something
else was growing.

40
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,280
When the plantations closed, the fields
were left empty.

41
00:02:16,320 --> 00:02:19,560
African grasses, brought in to feed
cattle, took over.

42
00:02:19,600 --> 00:02:21,720
Guinea grass. Buffel grass.

43
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:26,480
Molasses grass. Guinea grass can grow six
inches in a single day.

44
00:02:26,520 --> 00:02:30,520
When it dries out, it burns with flames up
to twenty feet high.

45
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:36,160
By 2023, Lahaina was a historic wooden
town, surrounded on three sides by

46
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:37,200
fuel.

47
00:02:39,540 --> 00:02:40,940
None of this was a secret.

48
00:02:40,980 --> 00:02:45,900
In 2014, a wildfire protection plan for
West Maui was signed.

49
00:02:45,940 --> 00:02:49,790
News reports say it rated Lahaina's fire
risk as extreme.

50
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:54,460
In August 2018, as Hurricane Lane
approached, a fire broke out in the

51
00:02:54,500 --> 00:02:56,830
Kauaʻula Valley, just south of town.

52
00:02:57,060 --> 00:03:01,900
It burned fifteen hundred acres and
damaged or destroyed twenty-one homes.

53
00:03:01,940 --> 00:03:06,790
A deputy fire chief called the conditions
unprecedented in the department's history.

54
00:03:06,980 --> 00:03:10,140
Years later, investigators found something
striking.

55
00:03:10,180 --> 00:03:14,620
The fire department and the police never
even started a formal review of the 2018

56
00:03:14,660 --> 00:03:18,780
fire. The emergency agency never finished
one.

57
00:03:18,820 --> 00:03:21,100
Then came August 2023.

58
00:03:21,140 --> 00:03:25,500
Far to the south, Hurricane Dora was
passing the islands at a safe distance.

59
00:03:25,540 --> 00:03:28,940
To the north sat a strong high-pressure
system.

60
00:03:28,980 --> 00:03:33,180
Between them, the air was squeezed into a
powerful river of wind.

61
00:03:33,220 --> 00:03:37,180
On August seventh, the National Weather
Service issued a red flag warning.

62
00:03:37,220 --> 00:03:41,180
Its wind warnings said the gusts could
blow down trees and power lines.

63
00:03:41,220 --> 00:03:44,510
The next day, gusts reached eighty miles
an hour.

64
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:51,600
Just after 6:30 in the morning on August
eighth, the wind brought down power lines

65
00:03:51,640 --> 00:03:54,680
near Lahainaluna Road, on the upper edge
of town.

66
00:03:54,720 --> 00:03:57,720
A resident named Shane Treu called 911.

67
00:03:57,760 --> 00:04:00,400
The lines had sparked the dry grass.

68
00:04:00,440 --> 00:04:03,360
Firefighters fought the blaze for hours in
the wind.

69
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:06,040
By around nine, it was declared contained.

70
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:10,560
At about 2:18 in the afternoon, after
nearly eight hours on scene, the crews

71
00:04:10,600 --> 00:04:15,440
left. The fire chief would later say it
had been fully contained and extinguished.

72
00:04:15,480 --> 00:04:18,480
Thirty-four minutes later, it was burning
again.

73
00:04:18,520 --> 00:04:23,360
Shortly before three, a Hawaiian Electric
crew working nearby saw a small fire, and

74
00:04:23,400 --> 00:04:27,650
called 911. Federal investigators would
call it a rekindle.

75
00:04:27,880 --> 00:04:31,120
Something in the morning burn area was
still alive.

76
00:04:31,160 --> 00:04:33,200
A spark carried by the wind.

77
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:35,920
Smoldering material moved by heavy
equipment.

78
00:04:35,960 --> 00:04:38,770
Or a root, burning slowly underground.

79
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:43,730
Even after a year of work, investigators
could not rule out any of the three.

80
00:04:46,380 --> 00:04:48,900
This time, nothing could stop it.

81
00:04:48,940 --> 00:04:51,460
The fire jumped the Lahaina Bypass.

82
00:04:51,500 --> 00:04:55,460
Around 3:30, the first homes on
Lahainaluna Road caught fire.

83
00:04:55,500 --> 00:05:00,580
At 3:30, Andaya, the head of Maui's
emergency agency, sent a text: closing

84
00:05:00,620 --> 00:05:02,340
Lahaina Bypass road.

85
00:05:02,380 --> 00:05:04,420
Fire jump over to the roadway.

86
00:05:04,460 --> 00:05:07,060
Police radio logs capture the speed of it.

87
00:05:07,100 --> 00:05:11,300
At 3:23, an officer reports blocking the
bypass into Lahaina.

88
00:05:11,340 --> 00:05:14,740
At 3:52: Front Street at a standstill.

89
00:05:14,780 --> 00:05:17,300
The wind had already knocked out cell
towers.

90
00:05:17,340 --> 00:05:21,700
Power was out. Many people had no idea how
close the fire was.

91
00:05:21,740 --> 00:05:24,180
Alerts did go out. A road closure.

92
00:05:24,220 --> 00:05:28,940
Then, at 4:10, an evacuation order for one
neighborhood at the top of town.

93
00:05:28,980 --> 00:05:32,980
But when investigators later interviewed
seventy-one residents, eighty-seven

94
00:05:33,020 --> 00:05:36,500
percent said they never received a single
text alert.

95
00:05:36,540 --> 00:05:40,140
At 4:24, someone reported fire near the
banyan tree.

96
00:05:40,180 --> 00:05:43,140
That is more than a mile from where the
morning fire began.

97
00:05:43,180 --> 00:05:47,100
At the same minute, the first death was
reported, on Mill Street.

98
00:05:47,140 --> 00:05:51,380
At 4:46, the fire crossed the highway into
the heart of Lahaina.

99
00:05:51,420 --> 00:05:54,980
Officers were breaking open locked gates
so residents could escape.

100
00:05:55,020 --> 00:05:59,100
Embers were flying ahead of the flames,
lighting roofs blocks away.

101
00:05:59,140 --> 00:06:01,460
And the roads out of town were jammed.

102
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:04,700
A fallen utility pole blocked one
direction of the highway.

103
00:06:04,740 --> 00:06:06,700
Traffic was turned away in the other.

104
00:06:06,740 --> 00:06:10,620
Survivors describe police cars and utility
crews blocking roads.

105
00:06:10,660 --> 00:06:14,900
The police chief later said officers never
stopped anyone from leaving.

106
00:06:14,940 --> 00:06:16,580
Accounts still conflict.

107
00:06:16,620 --> 00:06:18,870
But many survivors agree on one thing.

108
00:06:18,940 --> 00:06:21,940
The people who drove around the barricades
lived.

109
00:06:21,980 --> 00:06:25,910
Another said: the gridlock would have left
us there when the firestorm came.

110
00:06:26,060 --> 00:06:31,020
One survivor told reporters: we literally
had minutes, and one wrong turn.

111
00:06:31,060 --> 00:06:33,030
We would all be dead right now.

112
00:06:35,680 --> 00:06:38,890
For many people, there was only one
direction left.

113
00:06:39,120 --> 00:06:41,050
They ran toward the ocean.

114
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:43,000
Some sheltered behind the seawall.

115
00:06:43,040 --> 00:06:47,560
Others went into the water to escape the
heat and the smoke, as the town burned

116
00:06:47,600 --> 00:06:52,730
behind them. Around 5:45, the Coast Guard
learned that people were in the ocean.

117
00:06:52,960 --> 00:06:56,250
Boat crews from Station Maui spent the
night searching the smoke.

118
00:06:56,480 --> 00:07:00,320
They pulled seventeen people from the
water and helped forty more survivors

119
00:07:00,360 --> 00:07:05,720
ashore. At 7:19 that evening, Andaya told
state officials he believed only one

120
00:07:05,760 --> 00:07:07,400
business had been lost.

121
00:07:07,440 --> 00:07:10,280
By then, much of Lahaina was gone.

122
00:07:10,320 --> 00:07:14,250
The fire would not be fully contained for
twenty-six days.

123
00:07:16,900 --> 00:07:19,820
Herman Andaya was not on Maui that
afternoon.

124
00:07:19,860 --> 00:07:21,950
He was at a conference on Oʻahu.

125
00:07:22,020 --> 00:07:27,340
At 4:03, as the fire was moving toward
town, he texted: should I come home?

126
00:07:27,380 --> 00:07:31,100
Hawaiʻi has one of the largest outdoor
siren systems in the world.

127
00:07:31,140 --> 00:07:33,980
On Maui alone, there are eighty sirens.

128
00:07:34,020 --> 00:07:37,140
On August eighth, not one of them was
sounded.

129
00:07:37,180 --> 00:07:41,100
A week later, a reporter asked Andaya if
he regretted that decision.

130
00:07:41,140 --> 00:07:43,020
He answered: I do not.

131
00:07:43,060 --> 00:07:47,140
He explained that people are trained to
seek higher ground when a siren sounds.

132
00:07:47,180 --> 00:07:50,060
We just don't use sirens for fires, he
said.

133
00:07:50,100 --> 00:07:53,580
Had we sounded the siren that night, he
said, we were afraid that people would

134
00:07:53,620 --> 00:07:57,180
have gone mauka, toward the mountain, and
into the fire.

135
00:07:57,220 --> 00:08:00,340
The next day, he resigned, citing health
reasons.

136
00:08:00,380 --> 00:08:03,980
The investigation that followed found the
full story was worse.

137
00:08:04,020 --> 00:08:06,580
Nobody had even discussed using the
sirens.

138
00:08:06,620 --> 00:08:10,380
They had never been used for a wildfire,
not even in 2018.

139
00:08:10,420 --> 00:08:14,820
And of the five sirens around Lahaina,
only two were listed as working.

140
00:08:14,860 --> 00:08:17,900
They relied on a cell network that was
already failing.

141
00:08:17,940 --> 00:08:22,140
Investigators concluded that a siren might
not have told people what to do.

142
00:08:22,180 --> 00:08:24,990
But it might have given them one more
warning.

143
00:08:27,640 --> 00:08:29,360
And then there was the water.

144
00:08:29,400 --> 00:08:33,760
As firefighters pulled hoses into the
streets, the hydrants began to lose

145
00:08:33,800 --> 00:08:38,680
pressure. One Lahaina firefighter put it
simply: there was just no water in the

146
00:08:38,720 --> 00:08:42,320
hydrants. It wasn't that Lahaina had run
out of water.

147
00:08:42,360 --> 00:08:44,400
The system was bleeding.

148
00:08:44,440 --> 00:08:48,800
Inside every burning house, plastic pipes
melted and broke open.

149
00:08:48,840 --> 00:08:53,040
Water poured out of burning homes all over
town, and the pressure in the hydrants

150
00:08:53,080 --> 00:08:56,770
collapsed. From the air, help was
impossible.

151
00:08:56,920 --> 00:08:59,970
The gusts were too strong for helicopters
to drop water.

152
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:05,170
On the ground, firefighters were fighting
a firestorm, with almost nothing.

153
00:09:07,820 --> 00:09:09,240
So who was responsible?

154
00:09:09,280 --> 00:09:12,207
The answer that came back was not one
person.

155
00:09:12,247 --> 00:09:17,700
It was a system. Hawaiian Electric had no
program to shut off power before dangerous

156
00:09:17,740 --> 00:09:22,900
winds. Its chief executive told Congress
such shutoffs were not an appropriate fit

157
00:09:22,940 --> 00:09:27,780
for Hawaiʻi. In October 2024, the official
cause report ruled the fire

158
00:09:27,820 --> 00:09:32,980
accidental. Broken, re-energized power
lines had started the morning fire.

159
00:09:33,020 --> 00:09:36,580
The afternoon fire was that same fire,
rekindled.

160
00:09:36,620 --> 00:09:40,340
The lead federal agent said of the
firefighters: I do believe they did

161
00:09:40,380 --> 00:09:41,833
everything possible.

162
00:09:41,873 --> 00:09:46,020
Hawaiian Electric said it deeply regretted
that its operations contributed to the

163
00:09:46,060 --> 00:09:47,780
fire that ignited in the morning.

164
00:09:47,820 --> 00:09:50,767
The state's independent investigation went
further.

165
00:09:50,807 --> 00:09:55,180
It listed eighty-four findings, and
concluded that no single factor caused the

166
00:09:55,220 --> 00:09:59,260
disaster. The attorney general called it a
wake-up call for the state and county

167
00:09:59,300 --> 00:10:02,260
governments. Warnings that were never
studied.

168
00:10:02,300 --> 00:10:05,540
A fire that came back after the crews had
gone.

169
00:10:05,580 --> 00:10:07,380
Alerts that never arrived.

170
00:10:07,420 --> 00:10:09,380
Sirens nobody considered.

171
00:10:09,420 --> 00:10:10,820
Roads that clogged.

172
00:10:10,860 --> 00:10:12,740
Hydrants that ran dry.

173
00:10:12,780 --> 00:10:16,500
As the head of the institute that led the
investigation put it: the conditions that

174
00:10:16,540 --> 00:10:19,780
made this tragedy possible were years in
the making.

175
00:10:19,820 --> 00:10:25,300
In 2024, seven defendants, including the
state, Maui County and Hawaiian Electric,

176
00:10:25,340 --> 00:10:28,740
agreed to a settlement of more than four
billion dollars.

177
00:10:28,780 --> 00:10:33,167
Hawaiian Electric now has a program to cut
the power when dangerous winds arrive.

178
00:10:33,207 --> 00:10:35,593
It started in July 2024.

179
00:10:35,633 --> 00:10:38,870
The company calls it a last line of
defense.

180
00:10:41,520 --> 00:10:44,810
One hundred and two people died in the
Lahaina fire.

181
00:10:45,040 --> 00:10:46,730
The youngest was seven.

182
00:10:46,960 --> 00:10:49,320
The oldest was ninety-seven.

183
00:10:49,360 --> 00:10:52,880
Most were older; the median age was sixty-
eight.

184
00:10:52,920 --> 00:10:56,250
At least sixty-five of them were trying to
get out.

185
00:10:56,320 --> 00:10:57,960
Some were found in their homes.

186
00:10:58,000 --> 00:10:59,480
Some in their cars.

187
00:10:59,520 --> 00:11:02,280
Some outdoors, in the streets they knew.

188
00:11:02,320 --> 00:11:05,000
Eighty-two of them died in central
Lahaina.

189
00:11:05,040 --> 00:11:08,200
Seven died at a single senior housing
complex.

190
00:11:08,240 --> 00:11:11,720
More than two thousand buildings were
damaged or destroyed.

191
00:11:11,760 --> 00:11:13,560
Most of them were homes.

192
00:11:13,600 --> 00:11:17,720
At one point, three hundred and eighty-
eight people were listed as missing.

193
00:11:17,760 --> 00:11:22,600
The last victim died in March 2024, seven
months after the fire.

194
00:11:22,640 --> 00:11:26,730
Nearly eight thousand people spent months
living in hotels.

195
00:11:26,980 --> 00:11:29,230
[Tom Liu:] It was nothing, nothing,
nothing left.

196
00:11:29,560 --> 00:11:32,880
Three years later, Lahaina is still
rebuilding.

197
00:11:32,920 --> 00:11:36,720
By August 2026, a few hundred homes had
been rebuilt.

198
00:11:36,760 --> 00:11:40,450
On historic Front Street, rebuilding had
barely begun.

199
00:11:40,700 --> 00:11:47,350
[Tom Liu:] I'm 82, and it's... why, why
rebuild? It probably will take at least
two years.

200
00:11:47,640 --> 00:11:51,440
But in the old town square, the banyan
tree is still standing.

201
00:11:51,480 --> 00:11:53,680
Half of its branches died in the fire.

202
00:11:53,720 --> 00:11:55,770
The other half is growing back.

203
00:11:56,620 --> 00:12:00,310
The Lahaina fire is often described as a
natural disaster.

204
00:12:00,460 --> 00:12:03,110
A freak wind. A perfect storm.

205
00:12:03,340 --> 00:12:06,260
But almost every part of it had been seen
before.

206
00:12:06,300 --> 00:12:08,740
The dry grass was on the hills for years.

207
00:12:08,780 --> 00:12:11,540
The 2018 fire burned right next door.

208
00:12:11,580 --> 00:12:14,100
The wind was forecast days in advance.

209
00:12:14,140 --> 00:12:16,100
The sirens were on their poles.

210
00:12:16,140 --> 00:12:18,020
The fire was declared out.

211
00:12:18,060 --> 00:12:19,830
And the crews went home.

212
00:12:19,900 --> 00:12:23,260
Lahaina did not burn because nobody could
see the danger.

213
00:12:23,300 --> 00:12:27,180
It burned because so many small warnings
were never put together.

214
00:12:27,220 --> 00:12:31,030
And on August eighth, eighty sirens stayed
silent.

215
00:12:31,260 --> 00:12:35,140
If this story stayed with you, consider
subscribing, so more people hear how it

216
00:12:35,180 --> 00:12:37,350
happened. We'll see you in the next one.
